\subsection{Gradient Dynamics}

\begin{figure}[!htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/gradient_analysis_combined.pdf}
\caption{Gradient dynamics during VLA fine-tuning (preliminary analysis using v1 codebase checkpoint; seed 42, 200 batches at 50K checkpoint). (a)~Action loss gradient norms per encoder layer: Vanilla, Full OCC, and Random OCC are comparable, while Detach shows substantially reduced norms. (b)~Auxiliary loss gradient norms in the shared encoder (Detach excluded; no auxiliary gradients by design). (c)~Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity per layer (near zero for both Full OCC and Random OCC, which confirms orthogonality). (d)~Gradient norm ratio (Detach / Vanilla) per layer. V2 validation is ongoing; directional conclusions should be interpreted with caution.}
\label{fig:gradient_analysis}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis} examines gradient dynamics across conditions. All gradient norms are computed with respect to the L1 action loss (action tokens only), not the full language modeling objective.

\textbf{Gradient orthogonality.} Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity is near zero across all encoder layers for both Full OCC and Random OCC (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}c), with conflict rates of ${\sim}$50\%. This confirms that action and auxiliary gradients are orthogonal: direct gradient conflict is not the mechanism through which auxiliary heads affect safety. Action loss gradient norms are comparable across Vanilla and gradient-flow conditions, which rules out gradient amplification. Both patterns are identical for structured and random labels, consistent with the behavioral finding that spatial content does not affect outcomes.

\textbf{Mechanism remains open.} The observed interaction pattern---gradient flow is associated with lower safety, BatchNorm amplifies the deficit---cannot be explained by gradient conflict (orthogonality rules it out) or by gradient amplification (norms are comparable). The detrimental effect likely operates through indirect modification of the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape, but the precise mechanism requires further investigation.

\textbf{Representational structure is preserved across all conditions.} CKA between fine-tuned and pretrained encoder features shows that all conditions, including the worst-performing Full OCC (BN), maintain high similarity to the pretrained representations (CKA $\geq$ 0.95 for both DINOv2 and SigLIP encoders). The safety degradation under gradient flow therefore does not stem from representational corruption: the encoders retain their pretrained structure, yet the policy behaves less safely. This dissociation between representational fidelity and behavioral safety reinforces the conclusion that the detrimental effect operates through optimization dynamics rather than representation distortion.

Additional observations (per-task breakdown, depth comparison, and temporal dynamics) are in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.

